## 9. Conclusions

Across 22 clips and three VMAF targets, no single encoder wins everything. Each option has a clear strength and a clear cost.

**Vidaio is the most compression-efficient encoder tested.** It produces smaller files than every other option at matched VMAF — about 10% fewer bits than the FFmpeg open-source baseline on H.265, 9% fewer on AV1, and 15-43% fewer than the managed cloud providers (Bitmovin, AWS MediaConvert, Brightcove Zencoder). It is the only managed service in this benchmark that beats the open-source reference on compression. It hits target strictly on 68% of jobs, within 1 VMAF point on 95% of jobs, and within 1.5 VMAF points on 100% of jobs.

**FFmpeg matches Vidaio on reliability but not on operational profile.** FFmpeg strictly meets target on 100% of the matrix and handles content (e.g. grainy archive footage) that every cloud provider fails on. Achieving this requires running, scaling, and monitoring encoding infrastructure yourself, and wrapping the encoder with an iterative search loop to hit VMAF targets — the same kind of search loop that multiplies cloud providers' per-encode costs. Vidaio reaches comparable compression through a single API call with no iterative search and no infrastructure to operate. For teams with existing video-engineering capacity, self-hosted FFmpeg can be cheaper in absolute terms; for everyone else, Vidaio is the closest approximation of its efficiency through a managed interface.

**The cloud providers (Bitmovin, AWS MediaConvert, Brightcove Zencoder) lose on both axes that matter for a managed encoder.** They use 14-21% more bits than FFmpeg on H.265 and 7-59% more on AV1, while charging 2-36× more per encoded minute than Vidaio. None of them handles archive content. Their value proposition is managed-API convenience and existing enterprise relationships, but Vidaio delivers the same managed model with materially better compression and lower cost.

**Cost differences compound at scale.** Per-target price - what you actually pay to deliver one minute of source video at a target VMAF - ranges from $0.067/min (Vidaio, flat-rate, single pass) to $2.42/min (Bitmovin AV1, where the per-encode AV1 surcharge is 5× HEVC and the iterative search averages 4.8 passes). For a workload of 1,000 hours per year, that's the difference between roughly $4,000 and $145,000 in encoding fees alone, before storage. The published per-encode price is misleading because providers without native VMAF targeting need three to five search iterations to converge, each billed. FFmpeg has no list price; its real cost is the hardware and operations to host it.

